// `TypeEntry.name_id` does NOT always hold a name: register_array stores the array
// LENGTH there, and register_result stores the ERR TYPE ID. Scanning every type entry
// for a mangled generic name could therefore match one of those integers by coincidence
// and use it as the monomorphized type. Mangled ids land in the high hundreds even for a
// tiny program, so the `[i64; 974]` field below — next to a generic instantiation whose
// mangled name interned to exactly 974 — matched the ARRAY entry and SIGSEGV'd.
//
// CALIBRATION WARNING: the length 974 is tuned to this exact program's intern order. It
// bit at the time of writing (segfault before the fix, 43 after). If the compiler's
// interning changes, the collision moves and this test keeps PASSING while no longer
// testing anything — it degrades to a trivial program, never to a false failure. Do not
// read a pass here as proof the guard is alive; re-derive the colliding length (trace
// mangled_id at the scan site) if you need to re-verify. Spreading many array lengths
// does NOT help: each extra field interns another name and shifts the ids being chased.
struct Pair[T]:
    a: T
    b: T

struct Big:
    buf: [i64; 974]

fn main() -> i64:
    let p = Pair[i64](a: 3, b: 40)
    return p.a + p.b   // 43
